  "_meta": {
    "domain": "Include a person only if a historically significant part of that person's contribution is directly to computation or to an intellectual or technological foundation of computing: computing machinery and computer architecture; mechanical, electromechanical, or electronic digital computers; stored-program systems; programming languages and compilers; algorithms and data structures; computational complexity; operating systems and systems software; databases and data management; computer networking and distributed systems; artificial intelligence and machine learning; computer graphics and human-computer interaction; software engineering and program verification; cryptography and computer security; theory of computation, automata, formal languages, and computability; or mathematical logic and switching theory when the contribution directly established a foundation subsequently used to define or implement computation. Reject anyone whose principal significance lies in any other field. Reject mathematicians or mathematical logicians whose work has no substantive connection to computation; electrical and electronic engineers whose importance is principally semiconductor devices, circuit fabrication, telecommunications hardware, or electronics rather than computation; physicists whose principal contribution concerns physical theory or experiment; information scientists, librarians, documentalists, and bibliometricians whose principal contribution concerns information organization, scholarly communication, or library practice rather than computing; management scientists, business-computing specialists, and information-systems researchers without a substantive technical contribution to computing; statisticians whose significance lies in statistical theory or methodology without an algorithmic or computational contribution; linguists, psychologists, cognitive scientists, and neuroscientists whose principal contribution is to their own discipline rather than computational methods or artificial intelligence; and inventors, entrepreneurs, executives, or industrialists whose historical significance is primarily commercial, organizational, or technological rather than a contribution to computing itself.",
    "name": "computer science"
  }
